    "body": "### Expected behavior\n\nWhen a database connection dies while a transaction is in flight (e.g. a failover or network blip) and the subsequent rollback itself throws, ThreadLocalTransactionProvider should still clean up its thread-local state, so the next transaction started on that thread acquires a fresh connection from the ConnectionProvider and succeeds.\n\nThis is what happens for top-level transactions: DefaultTransactionProvider.brace(ctx, false) releases the connection and removes it from the configuration data map even when rollback throws, and the thread recovers.\n\n### Actual behavior\n\nIf a nested (savepoint) transaction is in flight when the connection dies, the thread is\npoisoned permanently: every subsequent transaction started on that thread \u2014 even days later,\nlong after the database has recovered \u2014 fails with:\n\n```\n    org.jooq.exception.DataAccessException: Cannot set autoCommit\n        at org.jooq.impl.DefaultConnectionProvider.setAutoCommit(DefaultConnectionProvider.java:207)\n        at org.jooq.impl.DefaultTransactionProvider.brace(DefaultTransactionProvider.java:226)\n        at org.jooq.impl.DefaultTransactionProvider.begin(DefaultTransactionProvider.java:145)\n        at org.jooq.impl.ThreadLocalTransactionProvider.begin(ThreadLocalTransactionProvider.java:93)\n        ...\n    Caused by: java.sql.SQLException: Connection is closed\n```\n\nThe thread never calls ConnectionProvider.acquire() again; only a JVM restart recovers it.\nIn our production incident (Spring Boot + HikariCP + PostgreSQL behind a Tomcat thread pool), one worker thread per instance stayed poisoned for 3 days, failing thousands of requests that happened to land on it. The pool had correctly evicted and closed the connection; jOOQ retained the closed proxy.\n\nCause, from reading the 3.20.6 sources (the relevant code is unchanged on current main):\n\n1. When the inner (savepoint) rollback throws (rollback-to-savepoint on a dead connection),\n   DefaultTransactionProvider.rollback performs no connection cleanup for non-top-level scopes\n   \u2014 brace(ctx, false) only runs when the savepoint stack is empty \u2014 so the inner transaction's\n   Configuration still holds the dead connection wrapper under\n   DATA_DEFAULT_TRANSACTION_PROVIDER_CONNECTION.\n2. In ThreadLocalTransactionProvider.rollback() (and commit()), configurations().pop() runs\n   after the delegate call, not in a finally \u2014 so when the delegate throws, that poisoned\n   Configuration is leaked permanently on the thread's localConfigurations deque.\n3. Every later ContextTransactional* call on the thread seeds from configuration(fallback) \u2192\n   peek() \u2192 the leaked Configuration. Its derived data map still contains the dead connection\n   wrapper (and cached autoCommit = true), so begin() \u2192 brace(true) \u2192 setAutoCommit(false)\n   fails on the closed connection. That failure's rollback also throws, pop() is skipped again\n   \u2014 the state is self-sustaining.\n\nNote: commit() intentionally skips pop() on failure because rollback() is expected to do it (per the #17517 comment) \u2014 but rollback() also skips it when the delegate throws, which is\nthe gap.\n\n### Steps to reproduce the problem\n\nNo code generation or DDL involved; reproduces with plain SQL on any table-less queries.\n\nOn a DSLContext configured with ThreadLocalTransactionProvider over a pooled PostgreSQL\nDataSource (HikariCP), using a single-thread executor to pin the thread:\n\n1. Submit to the executor:\n  ```\n ctx.transaction(() -> {                       // outer\n       ctx.transaction(() -> {                   // inner \u2192 savepoint\n           // read pg_backend_pid() and hand it to the main thread, then block\n           // until the main thread has killed the backend\n           ...\n           ctx.fetch(\"select 1\");                // fails: connection is dead\n       });\n   });\n```\n2. From the main thread (different connection):\n   `select pg_terminate_backend(<pid from step 1>)`\n3. The inner statement fails; the nested and outer rollbacks fail too (dead connection).\n4. Submit any new transaction to the same executor thread.\n\nExpected: step 4 succeeds on a fresh connection.\nActual: step 4 \u2014 and every retry on that thread, forever \u2014 fails with\n\"Cannot set autoCommit\" / \"Connection is closed\".\n\nControl: killing the connection in a top-level-only transaction does NOT reproduce the issue\n(the thread recovers), which isolates the problem to the nested/savepoint case.\n\n### jOOQ Version\n\njOOQ Open Source Edition 3.20.6 (relevant code verified unchanged on current main)\n\n### Database product and version\n\nPostgreSQL 17.9 on x86_64-pc-linux-gnu, compiled by Debian clang version 12.0.1, 64-bit\n\n### Java Version\n\nopenjdk version \"21\" (Eclipse Temurin)\n\n### JDBC / R2DBC driver name and version (include name if unofficial driver)\n\norg.postgresql:postgresql:42.7.7",
