    "body": "### Use case:\r\n\r\n`TransactionalRunnable`, `TransactionalCallable` and the methods that use them should be able to throw checked exception.\r\n\r\nJustification: It is common to read/write to the network or disk in the same scope as interacting with the database.\r\n\r\n### Possible solution you'd like to see:\r\n\r\nPossible options:\r\n* Add equivalent data structures that throw `<E extends Exception>`, or\r\n* Expose some finer-grained methods that are invoked by `DSLContext.transactional()` under the hood to enable users to build their own method that uses try-with-resources and throws whatever exceptions they see fit. \r\n\r\nI personally favor the second approach as it enables JOOQ to be used more like a library than a framework. It would look something like this:\r\n\r\n* Add `Transaction DSLContext.createTransaction()`, `Transaction.commit()`, `Transaction.rollback()`\r\n* Have `DSLContext.transactional()` invoke those under the hood.\r\n* Have user code do something like this:\r\n\r\n```\r\ntry (TransactionScope scope = new TransactionScope(dslContext))\r\n{\r\n  // do stuff\r\n  scope.commit();\r\n}\r\ncatch (IOException e)\r\n{\r\n  // do stuff\r\n}\r\n```\r\n\r\nWhere `TransactionScope`'s constructor invokes `DSLContext.createTransaction()` and its `close()` method invokes `rollback()` if needed.\r\n\r\n### Possible workarounds:\r\n\r\nTranslate `IOException` into `UncheckedIOException` and then back. Create similar exception wrappers for every different kind of checked exception that user code can throw. This is error prone and annoying to do.",
