    "body": "The existing transaction API is nice in most cases. But sometimes, a procedural approach is more desireable. The idea would be to have an API similar to JDBC or JTA:\r\n\r\n``` java\r\nTransactionalDSLContext tx = ctx.beginTransaction();\r\ntx.insert()...\r\ntx.update()...\r\nSavepoint savepoint = tx.savepoint();\r\ntx.delete()...\r\ntx.commit();\r\n```\r\n\r\nThis new API should integrate flawlessly with the existing API, and should adhere to the behaviour imposed by the `Configuration.transactionProvider()`\r\n\r\nAs a workaround, users can access JDBC directly, very easily:\r\n\r\n```java\r\n// Begin a transaction\r\nctx.connection(c -> {\r\n    DSLContext ctx2 = ctx.configuration().derive(c);\r\n\r\n    // Begin transaction\r\n    boolean autoCommit = c.getAutoCommit();\r\n\r\n    try {\r\n        c.setAutoCommit(false);\r\n        ctx2.insert()...\r\n        ctx2.update()...\r\n        Savepoint savepoint = c.setSavepoint();\r\n        ctx2.delete()...\r\n        c.commit();\r\n    }\r\n    finally {\r\n        c.setAutoCommit(autoCommit);\r\n    }\r\n});\r\n```\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\nSee also:\r\n- https://groups.google.com/d/msg/jooq-user/1JwWMChD2SM/NHUhSnI8AgAJ\r\n- http://stackoverflow.com/q/38134201/521799\r\n",
