    "body": "Apologies in advance if this isn't the right place to post this, since its more of a question than a bug.\r\n\r\nI'd like to run tests using Jooq against a real database and to automatically wrap each of these tests with a transaction. In JUnit 4 this is doable using a TestRule since this api allows you to wrap the execution of another test. JUnit 5 seems to have removed this capability. (https://junit.org/junit5/docs/current/user-guide/#extensions) As far as I can tell you can execute code before or after a test in several different ways, but there is no way to wrap the code. Because Jooq does not have a procedural transaction api, there does not seem to be anyway to use the two together (other than to manually wrap the body of every test in a transaction which is far from ideal).\r\n\r\nAre there any recommendations on how to get around this? \r\n\r\nI'm currently using jooq's ThreadLocalTransactionProvider, and it almost seems like I could call begin(), commit(), and rollback() directly. But all of these methods also take a TransactionContext and its not clear how to obtain one of those. It looks Jooq normally creates a `DefaultTransactionContext` in `DefaultDSLContext.transactionResult0` but `DefaultTransactionContext` is package private. Implementing and constructing my own `TransactionContext` to pass to the transaction provider seems to work, but I'm also a little worried that this might break in some unforeseen way. \r\n",
